    {
      "id": "claim_0d10ed2acfcabdd035be432d5f51b6810da68337c356cdd71b58f46fb3915236",
      "kind": "claim",
      "title": "Schroder-Bernstein theorem",
      "metadata_json": {
        "artifact_digest": "sha256:7d10b09e3d91a0348c97f931ca029df82b66baf46bdc9c76cadc8754d598d9a6",
        "artifact_path": "objects/sha256/7d/7d10b09e3d91a0348c97f931ca029df82b66baf46bdc9c76cadc8754d598d9a6.md",
        "bundle_digest": "sha256:51c08df27153e799469a100fffa5fd19fb49634cf9a1b73f12a11bfa46b6e36a",
        "bundle_entry_key": "thm-schroeder-bernstein",
        "bundle_id": "population.topology-analysis-logic-001",
        "claim_kind": "theorem",
        "conditions": [],
        "domain": "set theory",
        "draft": "claude-sub-topology/bundle_001.json",
        "evidence_ids": [],
        "logical_id": "math:logic.set-theory:schroeder-bernstein-theorem",
        "overlay_schema_version": 1,
        "proof_text": "Back-and-forth (chain) sketch. Trace ancestry: each a in A either lies in the image of g or not; if a = g(b), the element b is its parent, and b in turn may have a parent under f, and so on. Following parents as far as possible, every element of A or B falls into exactly one of three classes: its ancestor chain terminates at an element of A outside g(B) (an A-stopper), terminates at an element of B outside f(A) (a B-stopper), or never terminates (doubly infinite or cyclic). These classes partition A and B compatibly: f maps A-stopper elements of A bijectively onto the A-stopper elements of B (parents and images stay in the same class), g maps B-stopper elements of B onto the B-stopper elements of A, and f (or equivalently g^(-1)) matches the never-terminating classes. Define h from A to B by h(a) = f(a) if the chain of a stops in A or never stops, and h(a) = g^(-1)(a) if the chain of a stops in B (such a is in the image of g, since a itself is not a stopper of A, so g^(-1)(a) is defined; injectivity of g makes it unique). Each piece of h is a bijection between the corresponding classes, and the classes partition both sets, so h is a bijection from A onto B.",
        "release_manifest_digest": "sha256:81845b66a64fd9e8baa9830788af1d24ccfa7255e76ef65a7c1032858629b23b",
        "revision_digest": "sha256:0d10ed2acfcabdd035be432d5f51b6810da68337c356cdd71b58f46fb3915236",
        "source_id": "wikipedia:Schroder-Bernstein_theorem",
        "source_revision": "as-read-2026-08-02",
        "statement_fidelity_reviewed": false,
        "supersedes": [],
        "trust_class": "curated_secondary",
        "truth_status": "unverified"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_a0963439c3cff3c6dd93b06691ddf9ebaec29838699f770c302a9922f076ce0c",
      "kind": "claim",
      "title": "Uniform boundedness principle (Banach-Steinhaus)",
      "metadata_json": {
        "artifact_digest": "sha256:d3e3d7f6ed580e0188d55edcb50c37e7b866aa9671d45c7f3e42562be3db3306",
        "artifact_path": "objects/sha256/d3/d3e3d7f6ed580e0188d55edcb50c37e7b866aa9671d45c7f3e42562be3db3306.md",
        "bundle_digest": "sha256:51c08df27153e799469a100fffa5fd19fb49634cf9a1b73f12a11bfa46b6e36a",
        "bundle_entry_key": "thm-uniform-boundedness",
        "bundle_id": "population.topology-analysis-logic-001",
        "claim_kind": "theorem",
        "conditions": [],
        "domain": "functional analysis",
        "draft": "claude-sub-topology/bundle_001.json",
        "evidence_ids": [],
        "logical_id": "math:analysis.functional:uniform-boundedness-principle",
        "overlay_schema_version": 1,
        "proof_text": "Sketch via Baire. For each positive integer n let E_n = {x in X : ||T_i(x)|| <= n for every i in I}. Each E_n is closed, being an intersection over i of preimages of closed balls under the continuous maps x -> ||T_i(x)||. Pointwise boundedness says X is the union of the E_n. Since X is a complete metric space, the Baire category theorem forbids all E_n from being nowhere dense; some E_N has nonempty interior, so it contains a ball: there are x_0 and r > 0 with ||T_i(x_0 + z)|| <= N whenever ||z|| <= r and i in I. Then for ||z|| <= r, ||T_i(z)|| = ||T_i(x_0 + z) - T_i(x_0)|| <= 2N, so for any unit vector u, ||T_i(u)|| = (1/r) ||T_i(r u)|| <= 2N/r. Hence ||T_i|| <= 2N/r for every i.",
        "release_manifest_digest": "sha256:81845b66a64fd9e8baa9830788af1d24ccfa7255e76ef65a7c1032858629b23b",
        "revision_digest": "sha256:a0963439c3cff3c6dd93b06691ddf9ebaec29838699f770c302a9922f076ce0c",
        "source_id": "wikipedia:Uniform_boundedness_principle",
        "source_revision": "as-read-2026-08-02",
        "statement_fidelity_reviewed": false,
        "supersedes": [],
        "trust_class": "curated_secondary",
        "truth_status": "unverified"
      }
    },
